10 key takeaways
	1. Twitter is a read-heavy social networking system.
	2. Timeline generation is the biggest architectural challenge.
	3. Fanout-on-write improves read performance.
	4. Fanout-on-read avoids expensive writes for celebrities.
	5. Hybrid fanout combines both approaches.
	6. Kafka decouples tweet creation from timeline distribution.
	7. Redis provides fast timeline retrieval.
	8. Snowflake IDs help generate unique, sortable tweet identifiers.
	9. Inverted indexes make text search efficient.
	10. Eventual consistency is acceptable for home timelines.
	
	
Interview tip: Spend most of your time understanding fanout-on-write vs fanout-on-read. Be able to explain why a celebrity with millions of followers creates a completely different scaling problem from a normal user.


The central lesson: In Twitter, writing one tweet is easy. Delivering that tweet to millions of followers without bringing your infrastructure to its knees is the actual system design problem.
